<p>You do not need specialist knowledge of print quality to make the best 
 settings for your document. Use the following procedure to print a document 
 with the optimal settings for its type.</p>
<p>Depending on the printer you are using, this function may not be available.</p>
<p class="Note">Note:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If you select <span class="Term-Soft">DTP</span> 
 or <span class="Term-Soft">Photographic</span> in the <span class="Term-Soft">Standard 
 Settings</span> of the <span class="Term-Soft">Adjust Image Quality/Color</span>, 
 depending on the application you are using, images and graphics might 
 be printed in full color (CMYK), even in black and white documents. Characters 
 may also be printed using full color (CMYK) instead of pure Black (K), 
 as some applications send characters as image data to the RPCS driver. 
 </p></li>
</ul>
<ol>
<li><p>Open the printing preferences dialog box after 
 creating a document in an application.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Print Quality:Standard</span> 
 menu on the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>In the <span class="Term-Soft">Standard Settings</span> 
 of the <span class="Term-Soft">Adjust Image Quality/Color</span>, select 
 the icon that matches the kind of document you want to print.<br/>If you select <span class="Term-Soft">Advanced Settings</span> 
 for <span class="Term-Soft">Adjust Image Quality/Color</span>, you must 
 then manually set <span class="Term-Soft">Dithering:</span>, <span class="Term-Soft">Color Profile:</span>, and <span class="Term-Soft">Gray Reproduction:</span>.</p></li>
</ol>
<ol start="4">
<li><p>After making any other necessary settings, click 
 <span class="Term-OS">OK</span> to close the printing preferences dialog 
 box.</p></li>
<li><p>Start printing.</p></li>
</ol>
